From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lutvzn <106083845+lutvzn@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:54:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] qualcommax: ipq50xx: redmi-ax3000: add an overclock
 variant

A second image for the same board, xiaomi_redmi-ax3000-oc, whose CPU
clock is chosen in LuCI (System -> CPU Clock) instead of being fixed at
the rated 1008 MHz.

Its DTS adds 1056, 1104, 1152, 1200 and 1248 MHz at the same 1.1 V as
turbo-mode operating points. cpufreq-dt lists them as boost frequencies,
which stay unused until boost is switched on, so the image boots, and
failsafe runs, at 1008 MHz. The new luci-app-cpuclock keeps the choice in
/etc/config/cpuclock; at boot and on every config change its init
script switches boost on for a boost point, sets scaling_max_freq and the
performance governor, so the CPU runs at that clock. schedutil cannot be
used for it: it scales against arch_scale_freq_ref, the policy maximum
recorded at boot (1008 MHz, boost off), and caps the utilisation at full
capacity, so it never asks for more than 1008 MHz. Back at 1008 MHz,
boost goes off and schedutil returns. The page warns about overclocking
and, above 1200 MHz, that the tested board had no margin left. Its rpcd
ACL lists the thermal zones under both /sys/class/thermal and
/sys/devices/virtual/thermal: rpcd checks the resolved path too.

Measured on one IPQ5000 under network load plus a RAM tester: 1104,
1200, 1224 and 1248 MHz ran 15 min without an error; 1272 MHz cleared a
bit in RAM after 6 min and 1296 MHz reset the board within seconds.

The compatible stays xiaomi,redmi-ax3000, so board scripts are shared
and sysupgrade works between the two images. Before the flash write,
platform_pre_upgrade switches boost off, so the upgrade itself runs at
1008 MHz.
